  "bug_info": "Summary of configuration for Project: Csv\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    Script dir: /root/defects4j/framework\n      Base dir: /root/defects4j\n    Major root: /root/defects4j/major\n      Repo dir: /root/defects4j/project_repos\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n    Project ID: Csv\n       Program: commons-csv\n    Build file: /root/defects4j/framework/projects/Csv/Csv.build.xml\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n           Vcs: Vcs::Git\n    Repository: /root/defects4j/project_repos/commons-csv.git\n     Commit db: /root/defects4j/framework/projects/Csv/active-bugs.csv\nNumber of bugs: 16\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\nSummary for Bug: Csv-7\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevision ID (fixed version):\nce4e72701b1ad4caabc3bd668bd058fff082f2b6\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRevision date (fixed version):\n2014-05-04 16:22:34 +0000\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBug report id:\nCSV-112\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBug report url:\nhttps://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/CSV-112\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nRoot cause in triggering tests:\n - org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParserTest::testDuplicateHeaderEntries\n   --> junit.framework.AssertionFailedError: Expected exception: java.lang.IllegalStateException\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nList of modified sources:\n - org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------",
  "bug_report_content": "Title: HeaderMap is inconsistent when it is parsed from an input with duplicate columns names\nType: Bug | Priority: Major | Status: Closed | Resolution: Fixed\n\nDescription:\nGiven a parser format for csv files with a header line:\n{code}\nCSVFormat myFormat = CSVFormat.RFC4180.withDelimiter(\",\").withQuoteChar('\"').withQuotePolicy(Quote.MINIMAL)\n\t\t\t\t.withIgnoreSurroundingSpaces(true).withHeader().withSkipHeaderRecord(true);\n{code}\n\nAnd given a file with duplicate header names:\n \nCol1,Col2,Col2,Col3,Col4\n1,2,3,4,5\n4,5,6,7,8 \n\nThe HeaderMap returned by the parser misses an entry because of the Column name being used as a key, leading to wrong behavior when we rely on it.\n\nIf this is not supposed to happen in the file regarding the CSV format, at least this should raise an error. If not we should come up with a more clever way to store and access the headers.\n\nComments:\n\n[Benedikt Ritter]: The problem is, that we provide a key based access to the values of a {{CSVRecord}} using the {{get(String)}} method. How should that method behave if there are duplicate column names?\n\nThrowing an exception seems reasonable here.\n\n[Romain Goss\u00e9]: I guess handling this would imply too many changes in the API.\n\n[Benedikt Ritter]: Fixed in revision 1592371. An IllegalStateException is now thrown when an inconsistent header is parsed from the input.",
  "fix_diff": "--- org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser (buggy)\n+++ org.apache.commons.csv.CSVParser (fixed)\n@@ -29,6 +29,7 @@\n import java.net.URL;\n import java.nio.charset.Charset;\n import java.util.ArrayList;\n+import java.util.Arrays;\n import java.util.Collection;\n import java.util.Iterator;\n import java.util.LinkedHashMap;\n@@ -368,6 +369,9 @@\n             // build the name to index mappings\n             if (header != null) {\n                 for (int i = 0; i < header.length; i++) {\n+                    if (hdrMap.containsKey(header[i])) {\n+                        throw new IllegalStateException(\"The header contains duplicate names: \" + Arrays.toString(header));\n+                    }\n                     hdrMap.put(header[i], Integer.valueOf(i));\n                 }\n             }"
}